#X text 240 83 Grain properties:;
#X text 240 108 @at — grain position in the source array (in samples);
#X text 240 133 @l — grain length (in samples);
#X text 240 158 @amp — grain amplitude in the 0\..256 range;
#X text 240 183 @s — grain speed in the -10\..10 range;
#X text 240 208 @p — grain pan in the 0\..1 range;
#X text 240 233 @w — grain window: rect \, tri or hann;
#X text 240 258 @tb — time before grain start (in samples);
#X text 240 283 @ta — time after grain done (in samples);
#X text 240 308 @r — grain number of repeats (-1 means infinite);
#X text 240 333 @tag — grain tag;
#X text 240 358 How to choose a detection method: 'hfc' is best for
percussive sounds such as drums and other sharp transients. 'complex'
works well for tonal instruments like piano and strings \, where onsets
are less pronounced in amplitude but clear in phase and spectrum. 'specflux'
is a good universal choice when the source material is mixed or unknown.
'energy' is simple and fast \, suitable for signals with clearly separated
loud and quiet parts. 'phase' and 'wphase' are useful for sustained
or legato material where amplitude changes are minimal. 'specdiff'
\, 'kl' and 'mkl' provide alternative spectral measures and can be
tried when the default methods do not give satisfactory results.;
